<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="50–60 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 6</p>
  <h1>Present perfect <em>have / has + past participle</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">View a before-now event or situation from the present: <span class="ex">Rina has printed the signs, so they’re ready now. Maya has worked here since 2022. The team has welcomed eighteen visitors so far today.</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">50–60 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">before now · relevant now</span>
    <span class="tag">have / has + past participle</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Read a live status board beside its finished event log.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Find four ways a past situation connects to now.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Control participles, time viewpoint, adverbs, and duration.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Give a fictional event-status briefing and answer follow-ups.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can report a current result, describe experience up to now, show that a state continues, summarize an unfinished period, and switch to past simple for a closed event-time detail.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved a completed past event, present have agreement, and a neutral current-result statement.">
    <h3>Three checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the completed time, subject, or current description stated in each item.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="printed">printed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="print">print</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has">has</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are">are</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="were">were</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The event log gives a completed time yesterday: Rina <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="printed" data-hint="Yesterday places the completed printing event in a closed past frame." data-fix="Rina printed the first signs yesterday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the first signs yesterday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The singular subject Maya takes third-person present have: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has" data-hint="The singular subject Maya agrees with has." data-fix="Maya has a key." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a key.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The live status describes several signs now: The signs <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are" data-hint="The plural subject signs takes are for its current state." data-fix="The signs are ready now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> ready now.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice a live status beside a finished event log</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Harbor Center at 10:00 a.m.</h3>
    <p><b>Live status:</b> <span class="ex">Rina has printed the signs, so they are ready now. Lee has lost the storage key, so the door is still locked. Maya has worked at the center since 2022, and she still works here. The team has welcomed eighteen visitors so far today.</span></p>
    <p><b>Finished event log:</b> <span class="ex">Rina printed the first sign at 8:30. Lee lost the key at 9:15. Omar went to the supply store at 9:40.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which live-status sentence reports a result that matters now?</li>
      <li>Which situation started before now and continues?</li>
      <li>Why does the event log use past simple?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated a current result, experience, continuing state, unfinished-period total, closed event detail, and present-perfect structure.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify the time viewpoint</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Present-perfect meaning choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="current result">current result</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="experience up to now">experience up to now</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="continuing state">continuing state</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unfinished-period total">unfinished-period total</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="closed event detail">closed event detail</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have plus past participle">have plus past participle</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The signs are ready because Rina completed the printing: <span class="ex">Rina has printed the signs</span> presents a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="current result" data-hint="The completed action explains the signs’ present ready state." data-fix="Rina has printed the signs reports a current result." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A volunteer asks whether Maya has done this at any point before now: <span class="ex">Has Maya ever run a workshop?</span> asks about <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="experience up to now" data-hint="Ever asks whether the event occurred at any point in the period leading to now." data-fix="Has Maya ever run a workshop? asks about experience up to now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Maya started in 2022 and still works at the center: <span class="ex">She has worked here since 2022</span> describes a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="continuing state" data-hint="The work relationship began before now and remains true." data-fix="Maya has worked here since 2022 describes a continuing situation." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The event is still open at 10:00: <span class="ex">The team has welcomed eighteen visitors so far today</span> gives an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unfinished-period total" data-hint="So far today counts events inside a reporting period that is still open." data-fix="The team has welcomed eighteen visitors so far today gives an unfinished-period total." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The log locates the loss at 9:15 as one completed past event: <span class="ex">Lee lost the key at 9:15</span> gives a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="closed event detail" data-hint="At 9:15 anchors the event inside a completed past point in the log." data-fix="Lee lost the key at 9:15 gives a closed event-time detail." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The verb system in <span class="ex">They have welcomed</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have plus past participle" data-hint="Have is the finite helper and welcomed is the past participle." data-fix="They have welcomed uses have plus past participle." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: view before-now time from now</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The present perfect asks the listener to look backward from the present. The event or situation began before now, but the speaker’s chosen viewpoint includes now through a result, experience record, continuing situation, or unfinished reporting period.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>present connection</th><th>example</th><th>what matters now</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>current result</td><td><span class="ex">Rina has printed the signs.</span></td><td>the signs are ready</td></tr>
      <tr><td>experience</td><td><span class="ex">Maya has run three workshops.</span></td><td>three events belong to her record up to now</td></tr>
      <tr><td>continuing state</td><td><span class="ex">Maya has worked here since 2022.</span></td><td>the situation began then and remains true</td></tr>
      <tr><td>unfinished period</td><td><span class="ex">We have welcomed eighteen visitors so far today.</span></td><td>today’s count is open and can still change</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">The present perfect can include time information. <span class="ex">Since 2022</span>, <span class="ex">for three years</span>, <span class="ex">today</span>, and <span class="ex">so far</span> connect a before-now period to now. The rule is not simply “no time stated.”</p>

  <h2>Build the form: have or has + past participle</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>I / you / we / they</th><th>he / she / it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>positive</td><td><span class="ex">We have finished. We’ve finished.</span></td><td><span class="ex">She has finished. She’s finished.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative</td><td><span class="ex">We haven’t finished.</span></td><td><span class="ex">She hasn’t finished.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>question</td><td><span class="ex">Have you finished?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Has she finished?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>short answer</td><td><span class="ex">Yes, we have.</span></td><td><span class="ex">No, she hasn’t.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Have</span> or <span class="ex">has</span> carries subject agreement, moves before the subject in a question, and combines with <span class="ex">not</span>. Do not add <span class="ex">do</span> or <span class="ex">does</span>. Do not contract a positive short answer: say <span class="ex">Yes, I have</span>, not <span class="ex">Yes, I’ve</span>.</p>

  <h3>Past form and past participle are not always the same</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>base</th><th>past simple</th><th>past participle</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>work / print / finish</td><td>worked / printed / finished</td><td>worked / printed / finished</td></tr>
      <tr><td>go</td><td>went</td><td>gone</td></tr>
      <tr><td>see</td><td>saw</td><td>seen</td></tr>
      <tr><td>write</td><td>wrote</td><td>written</td></tr>
      <tr><td>take</td><td>took</td><td>taken</td></tr>
      <tr><td>do</td><td>did</td><td>done</td></tr>
      <tr><td>be</td><td>was / were</td><td>been</td></tr>
      <tr><td>leave</td><td>left</td><td>left</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You controlled helper agreement, polarity, question order, and regular and irregular participles.">
    <h3>Drill 1: complete the live status form</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Present-perfect form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has">has</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="haven’t">haven’t</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hasn’t">hasn’t</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Have">Have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Has">Has</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="written">written</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="gone">gone</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The plural subject volunteers takes have: The volunteers <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have" data-hint="The plural subject volunteers agrees with have." data-fix="The volunteers have arrived." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> arrived.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The singular subject Rina takes has: Rina <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has" data-hint="The singular subject Rina agrees with has." data-fix="Rina has printed the signs." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> printed the signs.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The team still needs three name tags: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="haven’t" data-hint="The subject we takes the negative form haven’t." data-fix="We haven’t finished the name tags." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> finished the name tags.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Lee still does not have the key: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hasn’t" data-hint="The singular negative with he uses hasn’t." data-fix="He hasn’t found the key." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> found the key.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Ask several volunteers about completion: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Have" data-hint="A question with the plural subject volunteers begins Have." data-fix="Have the volunteers moved the chairs?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the volunteers moved the chairs?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Ask about Maya’s experience: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Has" data-hint="A question about singular Maya begins Has." data-fix="Has Maya run a workshop before?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Maya run a workshop before?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>After <span class="ex">has</span>, use the participle of <span class="ex">write</span>: Rina has <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="written" data-hint="Write, wrote, written: written is the past participle." data-fix="Rina has written the welcome message." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the welcome message.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>Omar left and is still away at the supply store: He has <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="gone" data-hint="Go, went, gone: gone is the participle and signals that Omar has not returned." data-fix="Omar has gone to the supply store." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> to the supply store.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose the time viewpoint before the tense</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Use past simple when you locate an event inside a closed past time: <span class="ex">Rina printed the first sign at 8:30</span>. Use present perfect when the chosen frame reaches now: <span class="ex">Rina has printed all the signs, so they are ready</span>.</p>
  <p>A calendar word does not choose automatically. At 10:00 a.m., <span class="ex">The team has welcomed eighteen visitors today</span> treats today’s count as open. After the event closes, a report can say <span class="ex">The team welcomed forty visitors today</span> and present the day’s event as a completed whole.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>speaker’s frame</th><th>example</th><th>tense choice</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>result relevant now</td><td><span class="ex">Lee has lost the key, so the door is locked.</span></td><td>present perfect</td></tr>
      <tr><td>closed event time</td><td><span class="ex">Lee lost the key at 9:15.</span></td><td>past simple</td></tr>
      <tr><td>open count up to now</td><td><span class="ex">We have welcomed eighteen visitors so far.</span></td><td>present perfect</td></tr>
      <tr><td>completed event report</td><td><span class="ex">We welcomed forty visitors during yesterday’s event.</span></td><td>past simple</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">A definite closed event-time such as <span class="ex">at 9:15 yesterday</span>, <span class="ex">last year</span>, or <span class="ex">in 2019</span> normally selects past simple for that event. Ask a present-perfect experience question first, then use past simple for the finished detail: <span class="ex">Have you ever led a workshop? When did you lead one?</span></p>

  <h2>Place <em>ever, never, just, already, yet, since</em>, and <em>for</em></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>meaning and position</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>ever</td><td>at any time up to now; before the participle in an experience question</td><td><span class="ex">Have you ever led a workshop?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>never</td><td>at no time up to now; before the participle, without another negative</td><td><span class="ex">I have never led one.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>just</td><td>a very short time ago; between helper and participle</td><td><span class="ex">Rina has just arrived.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>already</td><td>earlier than expected; commonly between helper and participle</td><td><span class="ex">Lee has already called.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>yet</td><td>up to now; commonly at the end of questions and negatives</td><td><span class="ex">Has Omar returned yet? He hasn’t returned yet.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>since</td><td>starting point</td><td><span class="ex">Maya has worked here since 2022.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>for</td><td>duration</td><td><span class="ex">Maya has worked here for four years.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Never</span> already gives negative meaning, so say <span class="ex">I have never led one</span> or <span class="ex">I haven’t ever led one</span>, not <span class="ex">I haven’t never led one</span>. Ask duration with <span class="ex">How long have you...?</span></p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Natural U.S. English with <em>just, already</em>, and <em>yet</em></h3>
    <p>Present perfect is natural in U.S. English: <span class="ex">I’ve just sent it. Have you eaten yet?</span> Simple past is also very common with these words: <span class="ex">I just sent it. Did you eat yet?</span> Both patterns can describe a recent event connected to now.</p>
    <p>This lesson’s controlled items explicitly request the present-perfect form when that is the target. Outside those items, do not treat the common U.S. simple-past alternatives as errors.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3><em>Been to, gone to</em>, and <em>been at</em></h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Omar has been to the supply store</span> describes a completed visit and normally implies that he returned. <span class="ex">Omar has gone to the supply store</span> means he left and is still away or on the way. <span class="ex">Omar has been at the supply store for twenty minutes</span> places him there throughout a duration that continues now.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You controlled recent-event adverbs, duration boundaries, and the closed-time switch to past simple.">
    <h3>Drill 2: complete the present connection</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Present-perfect time choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="just">just</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="already">already</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="yet">yet</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="since">since</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="for">for</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="went">went</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The update arrived a moment ago, and the target form is present perfect: Rina has <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="just" data-hint="Just goes between has and sent to mark a very recent event." data-fix="Rina has just sent the update." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> sent the update.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The signs were ready earlier than the coordinator expected: Maya has <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="already" data-hint="Already commonly goes between has and printed." data-fix="Maya has already printed the signs." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> printed them.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The expected return has not happened up to now: Omar hasn’t returned <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="yet" data-hint="Yet commonly ends a negative present-perfect sentence." data-fix="Omar hasn’t returned yet." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The starting point is the year 2022: Maya has worked here <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="since" data-hint="Since introduces the point when the continuing situation began." data-fix="Maya has worked here since 2022." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> 2022.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The phrase three years states a duration: Lee has volunteered here <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="for" data-hint="For introduces the length of a continuing situation." data-fix="Lee has volunteered here for three years." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> three years.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The event log gives the closed time at 9:40: Omar <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="went" data-hint="At 9:40 locates the completed departure in a closed past point, so use past simple went." data-fix="Omar went to the supply store at 9:40." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> to the supply store at 9:40.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: weaken the helper, stress the result</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In positive statements, <span class="ex">have</span> and <span class="ex">has</span> commonly contract: <span class="ex">I’ve, you’ve, we’ve, they’ve, he’s, she’s, it’s</span>. Stress the participle or result: <span class="ex">Rina’s <strong>PRINT</strong>ed the signs. Lee’s <strong>LOST</strong> the key.</span> In an uncontracted question, the helper is often light and links forward: <span class="ex">Have-you ever <strong>LED</strong> a workshop?</span></p>
  <p>In common U.S. pronunciation, <span class="ex">been</span> often sounds like /bɪn/. The contraction <span class="ex">’s</span> can mean <span class="ex">is</span> or <span class="ex">has</span>, so use grammar and context. <span class="ex">She’s working</span> clearly means <span class="ex">she is working</span>; <span class="ex">She’s finished the report</span> means <span class="ex">she has finished</span> because <span class="ex">finished</span> heads the completed verb phrase and takes the object <span class="ex">the report</span>. <span class="ex">She’s finished</span> alone can be ambiguous without context.</p>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read current result, closed event detail, continuing state, unfinished total, been-gone status, and U.S. recent-event listening check">
    <h2>Listen: what connects to now?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional line once naturally and once more slowly. Listen for the time viewpoint, participle, contraction, and current implication. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard a current result, closed detail, continuing state, unfinished count, away status, and natural U.S. recent-event form.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="quiz-list">
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="the signs are ready now" data-hint="Has printed connects the completed action to the signs’ present status." data-fix="Rina has printed the signs, so they are ready now."><p class="quiz-question">1. What current result does Clip 1 establish?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the signs are ready now">the signs are ready now</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the signs were canceled yesterday">the signs were canceled yesterday</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the signs still need to be written">the signs still need to be written</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="9:15 is the closed event time" data-hint="At 9:15 places the loss at one completed past point." data-fix="Lee lost the key at 9:15, so 9:15 is the closed event time."><p class="quiz-question">2. Why does Clip 2 use past simple?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="9:15 is the closed event time">9:15 is the closed event time</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the key is a regular habit">the key is a regular habit</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the event is a future prediction">the event is a future prediction</button></div></div>
